ase don't leave your  database like hanging out there on aws  for hackers to find and shut your tools  down and then finally uh write a tech  conference talk to share what you  learned with the check  so here's our final project status um we  sent  not quite a million masks  we sent them to about six thousand  health care workers all around the  united states we built an entire  startup's worth of tools in about four  weeks  this is our final tech stack it is total  skunk works it was a frankenstein tech  stack but it did the job um it got us to  where we needed to go and at every stage  it enabled us to like move forward and  keep making progress and keep having  impact  great so  we want to look back and recap some of  the lessons that we hope you can take  away from this  in this framework  when you are  yourself trying to respond to a crisis  with technology try and think about  which one of these phases are you in and  what what problem are you trying to  solve so are you trying to validate your  idea are you trying to make it  repeatable are you trying to vertically  scale it  are you trying to stabilize it  or are you in the process of needing to  figure out should you adapt or should  you shut down our real hope for this is  that um not that you're going to go  through these exact stages as we did um  but we hope that by having some context  about what this could be like for you um  you feel a little more confident about  like saying yes um if someone comes to  you with an idea and you don't know if  it's a good idea but there's a crisis  out there and maybe your tech skills  could help um remember  there is there is a plan you can follow  our plan uh it's worth saying yes and  and trying  and of course we need to thank everyone  on this slide but especially liz klinger  our ceo and fearless leader uh  you can't build tools for nobody so  obviously our volunteers who actually  did all the work of mass match and then  of course our volunteers can't send mass  if there aren't masks to send so the  seven thousand masked donors who uh  reached into their closets to help out  with that  thank you  okay so the question was uh what did we  have this framework at the time or did  we come up with it retrospectively and  what were the what were the resources at  the time  uh we did not have this at the time  yeah this is all retrospective um i  don't think i mean we don't have like an  appendix slide with great resources a  book that i desperately that i read  sense that i desperately wish i had read  before is a book called uh mutual aid by  dean spade um if you are interested in  crisis response cannot recommend it  enough um it touches on a lot of the  operational and equity pieces that we  did not have time to get into in this  talk um but we would love to talk about  at any point as well we could write an  entire talk just on that we also were  the sort of coo  role for this organization as well  managing all the volunteers  yeah the question is like how do you  practice for a situation like this um  we had done a hackathon together  previously it was not good practice for  this um i think um  part of the difficulty of this and the  reason that our framework is a little  vague is that it's gonna be very  purpose-built around the problem you're  trying to solve  i don't know that  i i mean i think that  maybe doing something like setting up a  conference would help out with that um  so  or i don't know also if you can work on  internal tools within your organization  i came from a background of doing  internal tools so i was familiar with bi  tools and you know ritual had come  across at some point as well  that's a way of doing it it might be a  too big of a change but maybe doing  something like a conference or trying to  set up those um clicking those systems  together  could do that  yeah  but also you'll figure it out uh you  know as long as you have as long as you  have a good tech team like it's a bunch  of people smart people in a room  together like really motivated you will  you'll be fine  so the question is were there any  security concerns  and how do we handle those especially  around emails  certainly oh god there were so many  security concerns um we we tried to  mitigate it as best we did as best we  could um a lot of our negations were  very operational so um it was like we  locked down like no one could export the  whole google sheet for example we did a  lot of like permissioning stuff we also  had like a volunteer code of conduct  that's spelled out really specifically  um you will never export any data  outside of our systems and you will not  contact people except like you know oh i  just want to talk to this person no yeah  yeah so there was the policy and then  there was pulling it out of google  sheets as soon as we could as well i  think into retool yeah and yeah moving  to retail but also the move to postgres  was definitely like something like that  was part of our concern was like okay we  have so many volunteers now we are not  personally we have volunteer captains  we're on boarding people but we're no  longer personally vetting volunteers um  yeah i wish i had like a great answer  about like we did these smart things for  security um but i think at the time we  were like we just want to trust that our  volunteers are here to help um and we're  going to make a code of conduct that  allows us to eject people who abuse  anything  yeah  so the question is did we have to deal  with abusers and trolls  there were a couple people who said oh  i'm a health care worker i need 10 000  masks and they were usually pretty easy  to spot  it's possible that some people got  through but that's why we started uh  really verifying people pretty closely  and we  we've revamped that and made it a little  harder um to prove that you were a  healthcare worker um  a couple of days into it yeah also most  people who were in were saying like i  need 50 masks um i think just the scale  of donations we were providing was not  enough for us to be a target and anyone  who said like i need ten thousand mask  you're automatically like  um i think on the flip side in our  volunteer community um we did not have a  problem with  like within the community people being  horrible to each other which is really  lucky we also said like no volunteers  who are like we got a bunch of emails  from people who are like high schoolers  who are doing like you know school from  home and we're like i want to help out  and we said no one under 18. um we also  said like no volunteers who don't  already know how to use google sheets  and slack and so that mostly meant we  were getting people who were like  professional age and who were used to  like working together with other people  in a virtual space and so that i think  just like made it a little easier for us  yeah cool okay maybe one more question  and then we'll let you all go to lunch  yeah  yeah the question is like some of our  stuff is not free how do we pay for it  uh which is a great question um we did  get donations um we asked for we like  set up a gofundme to get like three  thousand dollars to pay for like that'll  basically fund us forever and then we  got way more money um a great thing i  mean  this is a horrible thing to say in a  crisis situation people want to help and  giving you money is a really easy way  for people to help and so um some of the  things like you know we implemented free  scout which was free and it was great if  we had just paid for zendesk would that  have saved us any time um you're gonna  get money from people like most likely  and again like you're not trying to run  this forever so even if you are like  if we had paid all our twilio costs out  of pocket i think we would have spent  like  345 dollars um so  it's just not an it's it's not  largely enough money that you couldn't  just pay for stuff to make your life  easier and in fact i think we wish we  had just paid for more stuff to make our  lives easier or just asked for more free  stuff so retool gave us free stuff y  combinator the accelerator in san  francisco they just said here's ten  thousand dollars go spend it on aws  whatever you want so postgres like was  running on a server that was paid by  them um i think squarespace gave us that  right give us our website for free so  everyone was super generous at the time  um and and we appreciate that yeah  cool well thank you for coming to our  talk and uh  [Music]  you
 

